  "source": {
    "display_title": "Compiler Design for Distributed Quantum Computing",
    "authors": [
      "Davide Ferrari",
      "Angela Sara Cacciapuoti",
      "Michele Amoretti",
      "Marcello Caleffi"
    ],
    "source_id": "source-03-compiler-design-for-distributed-quantum-computing--64a02810764d",
    "doi": "10.1109/TQE.2021.3053921",
    "venue": "IEEE Transactions on Quantum Engineering"
  },
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Distributed quantum computing can let remote quantum processing units cooperate as a virtual quantum processor for tasks that individual noisy intermediate-scale quantum devices cannot handle alone.",
      "short_title": "Distributed QPUs cooperate virtually",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Remote quantum processing units can communicate and cooperate for executing computational tasks that single, noisy, intermediate-scale quantum devices cannot handle by themselves."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Distributed quantum computing requires compilers that can map arbitrary quantum algorithms to distributed architectures while accounting for network and communication constraints.",
      "short_title": "Distributed architectures require compilers",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Distributed quantum computing requires a new generation of quantum compilers, for mapping any quantum algorithm to any distributed quantum computing architecture."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Remote CNOT execution consumes Bell-state resources and is limited by the number of available communication qubits, creating a tradeoff between communication capacity and data-qubit capacity.",
      "short_title": "Remote CNOTs consume Bell states",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "For each remote CNOT, a Bell state is consumed and a new Bell state must be distributed between the remote processors through the quantum link before another remote CNOT can be executed.",
        "The number of remote CNOTs that can be executed simultaneously between two processors is limited by the number of communication qubits jointly available at each processor."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Distributed quantum compilers must model temporal coupling-map dynamics because remote operations are constrained by Bell-state availability, Bell-state regeneration, and classical communication latency.",
      "short_title": "Coupling maps become dynamic",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "These additional constraints must be properly modeled within the coupling map, so that the distributed quantum compiler can optimize the quantum circuit by accounting for the temporal dynamics arising with the distributed architecture.",
        "The clock of the remote operations will be significantly lower than the clock of the local operations."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Entanglement swapping augments distributed-processor connectivity by enabling remote CNOTs between processors that are not directly connected by a quantum link, but it increases EPR-pair consumption at intermediate processors.",
      "short_title": "Entanglement swapping augments connectivity",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Entanglement swapping enhances the network connectivity through virtual quantum links.",
        "Entanglement swapping consumes the Bell states stored within the communication qubits at the intermediate processors."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The paper's worst-case system model uses one data qubit per processor and a one-dimensional nearest-neighbor processor topology, making every CNOT remote and maximizing distributed-computation overhead.",
      "short_title": "Worst case maximizes overhead",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Only one data qubit is available at each quantum processor.",
        "The quantum processors are interconnected through a 1-D nearest-neighbor topology, and only one data qubit is available at each quantum processor."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "In the worst-case topology, the entanglement-swapping-based strategy has per-layer depth overhead bounded by n/2 times a hardware-dependent constant des, where des equals cle plus cbsm plus ccx.",
      "short_title": "Entanglement strategy has linear bound",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "The depth overhead of the entanglement-swapping-based strategy does not exceed the following depth: n/2 des.",
        "des = cle + cbsm + ccx."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The data-qubit-swapping-based strategy arranges data qubits so each CNOT in a layer operates on neighboring processors, and its compiled per-layer depth does not exceed n/4 dqs plus dqs prime.",
      "short_title": "Data swapping lowers asymptotic overhead",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "The objective is to arrange (i.e., to swap) the data qubits within the quantum processors so that eventually each CNOT of the original layer operates on qubits stored at neighbor processors within the network topology.",
        "Theorem 1: Let us consider the ith layer of an arbitrary n-qubit quantum circuit. The depth of the corresponding compiled quantum circuit, obtained through the data-qubit-swapping-based strategy, does not exceed the following depth: n/4 dqs + dqs'."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The proposed compiler is computationally efficient because distributing a circuit has O(d n squared) complexity, with d the circuit depth and n the number of QPUs or logical qubits in the modeled worst case.",
      "short_title": "Compiler complexity is polynomial",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Applying a remote CNOT has a computational complexity of O(n), with n being the number of QPUs. Given that we need at most n/4 swaps, the computational complexity of Algorithm 2 turns out to be O(n2). The overall computational complexity of distributing a circuit is, therefore, O(dn2)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Experimental validation on medium-size quantum circuits respected the analytical bounds, and the data-qubit-swapping strategy generally required fewer link-generation layers and lower circuit depth than entanglement swapping alone.",
      "short_title": "Experiments respect analytical bounds",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Table 1 clearly shows that the upper bounds on the number of layers that result from compiling the layers of remote CNOTs are widely respected and hold for all the considered examples.",
        "Fig. 15(c) also shows that choosing the data-qubit-swapping-based strategy against the entanglement-swapping-based strategy is generally the best choice.",
        "Fig. 16(c) confirms that the data-qubit-swapping-based strategy is better than the entanglement-swapping-based one."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_items": [
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-remote-cnot-bell-state-protocol--7a3f2c9b4d81",
      "title": "Remote CNOT Bell-State Protocol",
      "excerpt": "Remote CNOT. To perform a CNOT between remote physical qubits stored at different processors, a Bell state such as Phi+ must be distributed through the quantum link so that each pair member is stored within a communication qubit at each processor. Once the Bell state is available, the remote CNOT is obtained with a local CNOT between the data and the communication qubit at each processor, followed by a conditional gate on the data qubit depending on the measurement of the remote communication qubit.",
      "overview": "This figure-level construction is the operational foundation for the compiler problem. It establishes why distributed gates require communication qubits, Bell-state generation, local gates, measurement, and classical feed-forward, so remote CNOTs are not interchangeable with local CNOTs in cost or scheduling.",
      "evidence_type": "figure_result",
      "claim_refs": [
        "2",
        "3"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-03-compiler-design-for-distributed-quantum-computing--64a02810764d",
      "page_refs": [
        "Fig. 4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-dynamic-coupling-map-equation--2c61e90ab7f4",
      "title": "Dynamic Coupling Map Model",
      "excerpt": "Formally, a coupling map is a visual representation of the directed graph G = (V, E), where V = {vi} denotes the set of vertices representing the qubits and E = {ei,j} denotes the set of directed edges representing the possibility to perform a CNOT, with vi and vj acting as control and target qubit, respectively.",
      "overview": "The coupling-map equation supplies the formal object that distributed compilation must extend. The paper uses it to argue that a static graph is insufficient once remote operations depend on Bell-state availability and regeneration timing, motivating dynamic scheduling constraints.",
      "evidence_type": "equation",
      "claim_refs": [
        "3"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-03-compiler-design-for-distributed-quantum-computing--64a02810764d",
      "page_refs": [
        "Eq. 1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-entanglement-swapping-connectivity--91b4ef03c6a2",
      "title": "Entanglement Swapping Connectivity Gain",
      "excerpt": "Entanglement swapping enhances the network connectivity through virtual quantum links. The number of additional interactions via entanglement swapping scales linearly with the number of available processors when only two communication qubits are available at each intermediate processor. If this constraint is relaxed, the number of additional interactions via entanglement swapping scales more than linearly.",
      "overview": "This figure-backed reasoning explains the benefit side of the distributed architecture: processors that are not adjacent can still interact through virtual links. It also scopes the result by tying the scaling to communication-qubit availability and by noting the associated EPR cost.",
      "evidence_type": "formal_argument",
      "claim_refs": [
        "4"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-03-compiler-design-for-distributed-quantum-computing--64a02810764d",
      "page_refs": [
        "Fig. 6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-worst-case-lnn-topology--d8c0a5f7139e",
      "title": "Worst-Case Linear Topology",
      "excerpt": "The quantum processors are interconnected through a 1-D nearest-neighbor topology, and only one data qubit is available at each quantum processor. Whenever multiple data qubits are available at a single quantum processor, a local CNOT can be executed between these data qubits without incurring distributed-computation overhead; with just one data qubit available at each processor, each and every CNOT within the quantum circuit must be mapped into a remote CNOT.",
      "overview": "This model constraint makes the subsequent bounds conservative. By forcing every two-qubit interaction to cross processor boundaries and by minimizing topology connectivity, the analysis estimates overhead under deliberately unfavorable architectural assumptions.",
      "evidence_type": "model_constraint",
      "claim_refs": [
        "5",
        "6",
        "7"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-03-compiler-design-for-distributed-quantum-computing--64a02810764d",
      "page_refs": [
        "Fig. 7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-entanglement-depth-bound--5b7e1d2c8a0f",
      "title": "Entanglement Strategy Depth Bound",
      "excerpt": "The depth overhead of the entanglement-swapping-based strategy does not exceed n/2 des, where n denotes the number of logical qubits within the quantum circuit and des is a constant factor independent from the characteristics of the original quantum circuit given by des = cle + cbsm + ccx.",
      "overview": "The equations give an explicit linear-in-n worst-case upper bound for one strategy. They separate architecture-independent circuit size from hardware constants for link entanglement, Bell-state measurement, and remote CNOT execution after Bell-state distribution.",
      "evidence_type": "theorem",
      "claim_refs": [
        "6"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-03-compiler-design-for-distributed-quantum-computing--64a02810764d",
      "page_refs": [
        "Eq. 2",
        "Eq. 3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-sorting-network-depth--0f6a81d4b3c9",
      "title": "Sorting Network Depth Factor",
      "excerpt": "By taking advantage of the sorting network theory, it is easy to model the network topology constraints through the notion of insertion network, or equivalently bubble network. As a consequence, the overall depth of the equivalent quantum circuit grows with the number n of logical qubits as 2n - 3 instead of a logarithmic log n depth factor as for classical sorting.",
      "overview": "This equation-like model explains why moving data qubits across a nearest-neighbor quantum network has different depth behavior from unconstrained classical sorting. It underpins the paper's data-qubit-swapping strategy without pretending that arbitrary pair swaps are free.",
      "evidence_type": "equation",
      "claim_refs": [
        "7"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-03-compiler-design-for-distributed-quantum-computing--64a02810764d",
      "page_refs": [
        "Eq. 4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-data-qubit-swapping-theorem--a41c9e6b057d",
      "title": "Data-Qubit Swapping Theorem",
      "excerpt": "Theorem 1: Let us consider the ith layer of an arbitrary n-qubit quantum circuit. The depth of the corresponding compiled quantum circuit, obtained through the data-qubit-swapping-based strategy, does not exceed n/4 dqs + dqs', where dqs = 3(cle + cbsm + ccx) and dqs' = cle + ccx.",
      "overview": "The theorem is the paper's central analytical artifact for the improved strategy. It claims a smaller asymptotic factor than the entanglement-only approach, while keeping hardware-dependent costs explicit and preserving a worst-case interpretation.",
      "evidence_type": "theorem",
      "claim_refs": [
        "7"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-03-compiler-design-for-distributed-quantum-computing--64a02810764d",
      "page_refs": [
        "Theorem 1",
        "Eq. 5",
        "Eq. 6",
        "Eq. 7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-compiler-complexity-bound--6d9f30b2e8ac",
      "title": "Compiler Complexity Bound",
      "excerpt": "The compiler reads the circuit from left to right while updating the front layer, so its computational complexity is O(d), where d is the depth of the circuit. Applying a remote CNOT has a computational complexity of O(n), with n being the number of QPUs. Given that we need at most n/4 swaps, the computational complexity of Algorithm 2 turns out to be O(n2). The overall computational complexity of distributing a circuit is, therefore, O(dn2).",
      "overview": "This formal complexity argument supports the efficiency claim. It ties the proposed front-layer workflow to a polynomial bound rather than only reporting empirical behavior, although the bound is expressed for the modeled compiler and architecture assumptions.",
      "evidence_type": "formal_argument",
      "claim_refs": [
        "8"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-03-compiler-design-for-distributed-quantum-computing--64a02810764d",
      "page_refs": [
        "Algorithm 2",
        "Algorithm 3",
        "Algorithm 4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-validation-figures-table--c03e74a9b5d2",
      "title": "Validation Figures and Table",
      "excerpt": "Table 1 clearly shows that the upper bounds on the number of layers that result from compiling the layers of remote CNOTs are widely respected and hold for all the considered examples. Fig. 15(c) also shows that choosing the data-qubit-swapping-based strategy against the entanglement-swapping-based strategy is generally the best choice. Fig. 16(c) confirms that the data-qubit-swapping-based strategy is better than the entanglement-swapping-based one.",
      "overview": "The evaluation evidence connects the analytical model to compiled benchmark circuits. The extracted markdown preserves the qualitative conclusions more reliably than the table's numeric cells, so this item supports validation and comparative strategy choice but not a specific numeric effect size.",
      "evidence_type": "table_result",
      "claim_refs": [
        "9"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-03-compiler-design-for-distributed-quantum-computing--64a02810764d",
      "page_refs": [
